--- task
id: r01
dataset: reading
kind: lexical ranking with the score
answer: fewest

--- intent
Rank the documents by how relevant their body is to the text `$q` (BM25) and return the top 3 slugs with the score as `score`, best first.

--- params
{"q": "needle"}

--- edition2
query relevant($q: String) {
  match { $d: Doc }
  return { $d.slug, bm25($d.body, $q) as score }
  order { bm25($d.body, $q) desc }
  limit 3
}

--- edition3
query relevant($q: String) {
  match { from bm25({ match { $d: Doc } return { $d } }, $d.body, $q) as $score }
  return { $d.slug, $score }
  order { $score desc }
  limit 3
}

--- edition3
query relevant($q: String) {
  match { from bm25({ match { $d: Doc } return { $d } }, $d.body, $q) as $score }
  return { $d.slug, $score as score }
  order { $score desc }
  limit 3
}

--- expect ordered edition3
columns: ["d.slug","score"]
["d0",0.7411201885074448]
["d3",0.7186620009769161]
["d1",0.6587735008955066]

--- note
Recorded from the first edition-3 truth on #1316's head, 2026-11-19: the RFC types a `bm25` score as `F64` (`bm25_v1`), where the rows recorded from edition 2 held Lance's `F32` scores. The recorded documents and their order equal edition 2's rows; each score, rounded to `F32`, is one `F32` place from edition 2's (0.7411202, 0.71866196 and 0.65877354 there), since `bm25_v1` computes it in `F64`.
